# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines settings template
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   This file is a template for your settings. It holds no real passwords or
#   keys. To make your own settings file, copy this file to a file named
#   ".env" (with the dot at the start) in the same folder:
#
#     cp .env.example .env               (Linux, Mac, WSL or Git Bash)
#     Copy-Item .env.example .env        (Windows PowerShell)
#
#   Then open .env in a text editor and change the values that you need. For
#   the first start, you must change only POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
#
# HOW GU HEADLINES USES .env
#   Docker runs GU Headlines as five parts, called containers:
#     db       The database (PostgreSQL). It stores the stories.
#     web      The website that visitors see.
#     worker   The background program. It checks the news sites and the job
#              boards on a schedule.
#     caddy    The front door (Caddy, a free web server). It answers the
#              visitors on port 80 (http) and port 443 (https), and passes
#              each request on to web. It also gets the certificate for
#              https and renews it by itself.
#     backup   The nightly backup of the whole site (see "Backups" below).
#   Two more start only when you turn on your own email server (see "Your
#   own email server" below): mailserver (the mail server) and webmail
#   (read your mail in the browser).
#   When you start GU Headlines with "docker compose up", Docker Compose
#   reads .env. It gives each setting to the containers as an environment
#   variable (a named value that a program reads when it starts). The
#   programs read their settings only when they start. After you change
#   .env, apply the change with this command:
#
#     docker compose up -d
#
#   Docker restarts only the containers that need the new values.
#
# HOW TO WRITE A SETTING
#   - Write one setting on each line, in the form NAME=value.
#   - Do not put spaces before or after the "=".
#   - You do not need quotes, also for a value with spaces:
#       SITE_NAME=Guam News Now
#   - Lines that start with "#" are notes. Docker ignores them.
#   - Write a note on its own line. In a value, a space followed by "#"
#     starts a note, and Docker ignores the rest of the line.
#   - Do not use the "$" sign in a value. Docker Compose reads "$name" as
#     the name of a different variable and replaces it. For example, it
#     changes ab$cd to ab. If a value must contain "$", put the value in
#     single quotes:
#       POSTGRES_PASSWORD='ab$cd'
#   - For a yes/no setting, write true or false.
#   - To use the default of a setting, put "#" at the start of its line, or
#     delete the line. Do not leave a value empty, unless its note says that
#     empty is OK.
#
# KEEP YOUR .env PRIVATE
#   Your .env file holds secrets: the database password and the API keys.
#   (An API key is a password that a service gives to a program.) A person
#   who has a key can use that service as your site.
#   - Do not share .env. Do not show it in a screenshot or a chat message.
#   - Do not put real values in .env.example. That file is part of the code,
#     and everyone can read it.
#   - The .gitignore file tells Git to ignore .env, so "git commit" and
#     "git push" do not send it anywhere. The .dockerignore file keeps .env
#     out of the program image. Do not remove these rules.
#   - Keep a copy of .env with your backups. Without the database password,
#     GU Headlines cannot open its own database. (The nightly backups have
#     a copy of .env in them; see "Backups" below.)
#   - If other persons learn a key, make a new key at the service. Put the
#     new key in .env, and delete the old key at the service.
#
# HOW TO READ THE NOTES BELOW
#   Required   "yes" means that GU Headlines does not start without it.
#   Default    The value that GU Headlines uses when the line is missing.
#   Example    A value in the correct form.
#   Secret     "yes" means: keep the value private (see above).
#   More       The guide in the docs folder that tells more.

# =============================================================================
# Database
# More: docs/settings.md#database
# =============================================================================
# The database stores the stories, the job postings and the results of each
# check. Docker makes the database on the first start, with the three values
# below. After that, the database keeps those first values, also when you
# change them in this file. Set these values once, before the first
# "docker compose up", and do not change them after that.

# POSTGRES_DB
#   The name of the database.
#   Required: no. Default: guheadlines. Leave it as it is.
POSTGRES_DB=guheadlines

# POSTGRES_USER
#   The user name that the website and the worker use to sign in to the
#   database.
#   Required: no. Default: guheadlines. Leave it as it is.
POSTGRES_USER=guheadlines

# POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   The password of that database user.
#   Required: yes. Without it, Docker Compose stops with the message
#   "set POSTGRES_PASSWORD in .env".
#   Secret: yes. The value below is only a placeholder that everyone can
#   read. Replace it before the first start.
#   How to make a strong password: run "openssl rand -hex 24" and copy what
#   it prints. It prints only letters and numbers, so it is safe here.
#   Example: 3f9c1e0b7a... (48 letters and numbers)
#   You do not type this password anywhere else. Keep a copy in a safe place.
#   Do not change the password after the first start. The database keeps its
#   first password, so a new value here locks GU Headlines out of the
#   database. To change it safely, follow docs/settings.md#database.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# =============================================================================
# Web address and https
# More: docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet
# =============================================================================
# Visitors do not talk to the website (web) directly. They talk to caddy,
# which passes each request on to web.
#
# Without a domain name, the site works with plain http:// on port 80. Open
# it at http://localhost on this computer, or at the IP address of the
# server from a different device, for example http://203.0.113.5.
#
# For https (the padlock in the browser), the site needs a domain name, such
# as guamheadlines.com. https needs a certificate, and caddy gets one free
# from Let's Encrypt, but only for a domain name, not for an IP address.
# caddy renews the certificate by itself, so you do not have to.
#
#   How to turn on https:
#     1. Buy a domain name from a domain registrar (a company that sells
#        domain names, for example Cloudflare, Porkbun or Namecheap). A .com
#        name costs about 10 to 15 US dollars a year.
#     2. In the DNS settings of your domain at the registrar, add an
#        "A record". (DNS is the address book of the internet. An A record
#        tells it the IP address of a name.)
#          Type     A
#          Name     @   (the "@" means the domain itself, guamheadlines.com)
#          Value    the IP address of your server. Your server company
#                   shows it in its control panel and in its welcome email.
#        For www.guamheadlines.com too, add a second A record with the name
#        www and the same IP address.
#        On Cloudflare, also set "Proxy status" to "DNS only" (a grey cloud)
#        for each record.
#        Do not add an "AAAA" record (an IPv6 address) unless you know that
#        IPv6 works on your server.
#     3. Wait until the name points at your server. This takes from a few
#        minutes to a few hours. To check it, run this on the server:
#          getent hosts guamheadlines.com
#        It must print the IP address of your server. If it prints nothing,
#        wait and try again.
#     4. Make sure that ports 80 and 443 of the server are open to the
#        internet (docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet tells how).
#     5. Put the domain name in SITE_DOMAIN below, and apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     6. Check it: wait one minute, then open https://guamheadlines.com (your
#        own domain). The browser must show the site with a padlock. If it
#        does not, run this command and look for lines with "error":
#          docker compose logs --tail 30 caddy

# SITE_DOMAIN
#   The domain name of the site, without https:// and without a "/" at the
#   end.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (plain http:// on port 80, no https).
#   Example: guamheadlines.com
#   For two names, put a comma between them. Then both names show the site
#   over https, and caddy gets a certificate for each name:
#     guamheadlines.com, www.guamheadlines.com
#   Each name must already point at this server (steps 2 and 3 above).
#   Until it does, caddy cannot get its certificate. It tries again by
#   itself, and the site does not open over https or http.
#   When SITE_URL (below) is empty, GU Headlines tells search engines that
#   https://<the first name> is the address of the site.
#   Secret: no.
SITE_DOMAIN=

# HTTP_PORT
#   The port for http:// on this computer. A port is a number that tells
#   the computer which program gets a request. When an address has no port,
#   the browser uses port 80 for http://, so with 80 the address is simply
#   http://localhost or http://<server address>.
#   Required: no. Default: 80.
#   On a server with a domain name, keep 80. Let's Encrypt checks the domain
#   on port 80 or 443 before it gives a certificate, and caddy uses port 80
#   to send http:// visitors on to https://.
#   Change it only on a computer where a different program already uses
#   port 80. Docker then stops with "port is already allocated" or "address
#   already in use". For example, with HTTP_PORT=8000, open the site at
#   http://localhost:8000.
#   Only Docker Compose reads this setting. Inside Docker, caddy always uses
#   port 80, and the website always uses port 8000.
HTTP_PORT=80

# HTTPS_PORT
#   The port for https:// on this computer. When an address has no port,
#   the browser uses port 443 for https://.
#   Required: no. Default: 443.
#   On a server with a domain name, keep 443. Change it only on a computer
#   that does not use https, where a different program already uses port
#   443 (Docker then stops with "port is already allocated").
#   Only Docker Compose reads this setting.
HTTPS_PORT=443

# =============================================================================
# Website
# More: docs/settings.md#website
# =============================================================================

# SITE_NAME
#   The name of the site. It shows in the top bar, in the browser tab and at
#   the bottom of each page.
#   Required: no. Default: GU Headlines.
#   If you change the name, also change the picture that shows when a person
#   shares a link to the site (see docs/settings.md#website).
SITE_NAME=GU Headlines

# SITE_TAGLINE
#   A short sentence about the site. It shows at the top and the bottom of
#   each page and in the browser tab of the home page. Search engines show it
#   at the start of the description of the site.
#   Required: no. Default: Guam news, updated every hour
#   If you change SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES, change this text to agree with it.
SITE_TAGLINE=Guam news, updated every hour

# PAGE_SIZE
#   How many stories a page shows before the "Older" link.
#   Required: no. Default: 30.
#   Use a number from 5 to 100. GU Headlines uses 5 for a smaller number and
#   100 for a larger number.
PAGE_SIZE=30

# =============================================================================
# Search engines
# More: docs/maintenance.md#search-engines
# =============================================================================
# Fill in these values only after the site is on the internet (see
# docs/maintenance.md#putting-it-on-the-internet). While the site runs only
# on your own network, leave them empty. Empty is OK for all three.

# SITE_URL
#   The public address of the site. GU Headlines gives this address to
#   search engines for each page. It goes in the canonical link (the "main
#   address" of a page), in the sitemap and in link previews. A search
#   engine then lists each page only one time, under one address.
#   Required: no. Default: empty. Then GU Headlines uses https:// and the
#   first name in SITE_DOMAIN. If SITE_DOMAIN is empty too, each page uses
#   the address that the visitor used.
#   With SITE_DOMAIN set, you need SITE_URL only to choose a different main
#   address, for example the www name:
#     https://www.guamheadlines.com
#   Example: https://news.example.com
#   The value must start with https:// or http://. If it does not, GU
#   Headlines ignores it. A "/" at the end is OK.
SITE_URL=

# GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION
#   A code that proves to Google Search Console that the site is yours.
#   Required: no. Default: empty.
#   Where to get it: in Google Search Console, add the site as a "URL prefix"
#   property and choose "HTML tag". Google shows a tag like this one:
#     <meta name="google-site-verification" content="AbC123xyz">
#   Copy only the text between the quotes after content=, here AbC123xyz.
#   Secret: no. Each visitor can see this code in the page source.
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# BING_SITE_VERIFICATION
#   The same type of code, for Bing Webmaster Tools. Choose its "HTML Meta
#   Tag" option, and copy only the text between the quotes after content=.
#   Required: no. Default: empty.
#   Secret: no.
BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# =============================================================================
# Advertising (Google AdSense)
# More: docs/settings.md#advertising
# =============================================================================
# With both values set, the home page, the Jobs page, the Sources page, the
# Obituaries page and the visitor guides (/guides) show one ad each, at the
# top of the right-hand column.
# With ADSENSE_STORIES_SLOT set too, the home page and the Obituaries page
# show a second ad between the stories, and each visitor guide shows one in
# its text.
# With both values empty, the site shows no ad and loads no ad code. Empty is
# OK for both. To show the ad on another topic's page too, open
# config/categories.yaml, find the topic, and add the line "show_ad: true"
# under its name (lined up with "name:"), the same way Obituaries has it.
# The two values come from the ad code that AdSense gives you:
#   <ins class="adsbygoogle"
#        data-ad-client="ca-pub-1234567890123456"   <- ADSENSE_CLIENT
#        data-ad-slot="9876543210"                  <- ADSENSE_SLOT
# Copy only these two values. Do not paste the full ad code anywhere. The
# site writes the ad code itself, because its security rules block ad code
# that is pasted in.

# ADSENSE_CLIENT
#   Your AdSense publisher ID.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no ads).
#   Example: ca-pub-1234567890123456 (pub-1234567890123456 is also OK)
#   Where to find it: in AdSense, go to Account > Settings > Account
#   information. The "Publisher ID" line shows it. You can also paste the
#   line from the ads.txt page of AdSense, or its verification code. The
#   site takes the ID out of it.
#   With only this value, AdSense can verify the site, but the site shows no
#   ad. With this value, the site also serves the ads.txt file that AdSense
#   looks for. If the value has no publisher ID in it, the site acts as if
#   the value is empty, and AdSense says that it found no ads.txt file.
#   After you change this value, run "docker compose up -d". The automatic
#   update does not read a changed .env until the next update.
#   To check it: open https://your-domain/ads.txt in a browser (put your
#   own domain in). It must show one line, such as:
#     google.com, pub-1234567890123456, DIRECT, f08c47fec0942fa0
#   AdSense can take a few days to see a new ads.txt file.
#   Secret: no. It shows in the page source and in the ads.txt file of the
#   site.
ADSENSE_CLIENT=

# ADSENSE_SLOT
#   The number of your ad unit. To make an ad unit, go to Ads > By ad unit >
#   Display ads in AdSense.
#   Every page with an ad uses this same ad unit, so one number is enough.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no ads).
#   Example: 9876543210 (only digits)
#   Secret: no.
ADSENSE_SLOT=

# ADSENSE_STORIES_SLOT
#   The number of a second ad unit, shown between the stories: after the
#   fifth story on the home page and on the Obituaries page, when more
#   stories follow. On a phone, the right-hand column comes after all the
#   stories, so this is the ad that phone readers see first.
#   The same ad unit also shows inside each visitor guide (/guides), after
#   the guide's first part, when the guide has at least three parts (each
#   "##" heading starts a part; see docs/guides.md). Owner previews of a
#   guide never show ads.
#   How to get it:
#     1. In AdSense, go to Ads > By ad unit > Display ads.
#     2. Give the new unit a name that you will know again, such as
#        Between stories. Keep the shape "Responsive". Click Create.
#     3. AdSense shows the ad code. Copy only the number after
#        data-ad-slot= (only digits), and put it after the "=" below.
#     4. Run "docker compose up -d".
#   With its own ad unit, AdSense shows how much each spot earns: open
#   Reports in AdSense and choose "By ad unit".
#   This ad needs ADSENSE_CLIENT and ADSENSE_SLOT too. Like the other ad,
#   Google's ad code loads only when the visitor first scrolls, taps, presses
#   a key or moves the mouse, so the PageSpeed scores do not change. A new ad
#   unit can stay empty for up to an hour. When Google has no ad to show,
#   the block folds away.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no ad between the stories).
#   Example: 1122334455 (only digits)
#   Secret: no.
ADSENSE_STORIES_SLOT=

# =============================================================================
# Sponsor spot (an ad that you sell yourself)
# More: docs/settings.md#sponsor-spot
# =============================================================================
# Besides AdSense, you can sell one ad spot yourself, for example to a local
# business, and keep all of the money. The sponsor's picture is shown at the
# top of the right-hand column of every page, under the word "Sponsor" (on a
# phone, the column comes after the stories). A click on the picture opens
# the sponsor's website in a new tab.
#   - The picture is a file on your server, in the config/sponsor folder of
#     GU Headlines. The site serves it itself: no outside script, cookie or
#     request, so the pages stay as fast and as safe as before.
#   - The site makes a small copy of the picture (at most 600 pixels wide),
#     so a big file from the sponsor does not slow the pages down.
#   - With the visitor stats on (STATS_PASSWORD, below), the site counts the
#     clicks on the picture. The stats page then shows "Sponsor clicks", a
#     number that you can give the sponsor.
#   - The disclaimer on the Sources page gets a short paragraph that says
#     that the picture is a paid ad.
# Empty is OK for all three settings: then there is no sponsor spot.
#
# How to put a sponsor's ad on the site:
#   1. Get the picture from the sponsor: a JPG or PNG file. The best size is
#      600 pixels wide and 500 pixels high: the usual "300 x 250" ad, at
#      twice the size, for sharp phone screens. Other sizes work too.
#      Give the file a short name without spaces, such as ad.jpg.
#   2. Copy the file to the config/sponsor folder in the GU Headlines folder
#      on your server. To see the full name of that folder, run "pwd" in
#      the GU Headlines folder on the server; it shows something like
#      /root/GU-headlines. Then, on a Windows computer, open PowerShell in
#      the folder that has the picture, and run (with your own user name,
#      server address and folder):
#        scp ad.jpg root@203.0.113.10:/root/GU-headlines/config/sponsor/
#      It asks for the same password as ssh. A file-copy program such as
#      WinSCP or FileZilla (SFTP) also works, with the same address, user
#      name and password as ssh.
#   3. Put the file name in SPONSOR_IMAGE below, the sponsor's web address
#      in SPONSOR_LINK, and a short description in SPONSOR_ALT.
#   4. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   5. Check it: open your site. The picture is at the top of the right-hand
#      column. Click it: the sponsor's website opens in a new tab.
#      If there is no picture, run:
#        docker compose logs web | grep SPONSOR
#      The lines tell what is wrong (for example, no file with that name).
# To change the picture later, copy the new file over the old one, with the
# same name. It shows on the site within a few minutes, without step 4.
# To stop showing the ad, make SPONSOR_IMAGE empty and run step 4. The
# picture can stay in the folder.

# SPONSOR_IMAGE
#   The file name of the sponsor's picture in the config/sponsor folder.
#   Only the name, without the folder.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no sponsor spot).
#   Example: ad.jpg
#   Allowed: a .jpg, .jpeg, .png, .webp or .gif file (a GIF shows only its
#   first picture, without animation). Letters, digits, dots, dashes and
#   underscores in the name.
#   Secret: no.
SPONSOR_IMAGE=

# SPONSOR_LINK
#   The web address that the picture opens: the sponsor's website, or a
#   page on it, such as its menu or an offer. It must start with https://
#   (or http://).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (the picture is shown without a link).
#   Example: https://www.example.com/specials
#   Secret: no.
SPONSOR_LINK=

# SPONSOR_ALT
#   What the picture says, in a few words, for people who cannot see it
#   (screen readers read it out) and for when the picture does not load.
#   Search engines read it too.
#   Required: no. Default: empty, which gives "Sponsor: " and the name of the
#   sponsor's website (such as "Sponsor: example.com").
#   Example: Island Coffee: two iced coffees for 5 dollars, Hagatna
#   Do not use a "$" sign (see HOW TO WRITE A SETTING at the top of this
#   file); write "dollars" instead.
#   Secret: no.
SPONSOR_ALT=

# =============================================================================
# "Buy me a coffee" (reader gifts through PayPal)
# More: docs/settings.md#coffee-donations
# =============================================================================
# Readers who like the site can send you a little money, "a coffee", through
# PayPal. With a PayPal link set below, every page shows a "Support this
# site" box in the right-hand column, with a "Buy me a coffee" button. The
# button opens your PayPal page in a new tab. The reader picks the amount
# there and pays with a PayPal account or a card. PayPal sends the money to
# your PayPal account.
#   - The button is a plain link. No PayPal script, cookie or request is on
#     the site, so the pages stay as fast and as safe as before.
#   - The disclaimer on the Sources page gets a short paragraph that says
#     that the money is a personal gift, not a donation to a charity, so it
#     is not tax-deductible.
#   - PayPal keeps a small fee from each payment. The money is income for
#     you. Ask a tax preparer if you must report it.
# Empty is OK: then there is no coffee box.
#
# There are two types of PayPal link. Use type A if you are not sure.
#
# A. A PayPal.Me link (the easiest; it works with a personal account)
#   1. Open https://www.paypal.com/paypalme in a browser and log in to
#      PayPal.
#   2. Choose a name for your link, such as GuamHeadlines. Readers see this
#      name, and the name and the profile picture of your PayPal account,
#      but not your email address.
#   3. PayPal shows your link, such as https://paypal.me/GuamHeadlines
#      Copy it.
#
# B. A PayPal Donate button link
#   1. Open https://www.paypal.com/donate/buttons in a browser, log in, and
#      follow the steps to make a Donate button.
#   2. At the end, PayPal offers the button code and a link. Copy only the
#      link. It starts with https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=
#      Do not copy the button code (the text that starts with "<form" or
#      "<div"). The site makes its own button, and its security rules block
#      code that is pasted in.
#   With a personal account, PayPal can put your email address in the link
#   (business=you@example.com). The site does not use a link with an email
#   address in it, because the site never shows an email address. Then use
#   type A.
#
# Then:
#   1. Put the link after PAYPAL_DONATE_LINK= below, and save the file.
#   2. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   3. Check it: open your site. The "Support this site" box is in the
#      right-hand column (on a phone, after the stories). Click "Buy me a
#      coffee": your PayPal page must open in a new tab. If there is no box,
#      the link is not one that the site accepts (see "Allowed" below).

# PAYPAL_DONATE_LINK
#   The web address of your PayPal.Me page or of your PayPal Donate button.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no coffee box).
#   Example: https://paypal.me/GuamHeadlines
#   Allowed: a link on paypal.me or paypal.com. You can leave out the
#   https:// at the start (paypal.me/GuamHeadlines also works). A link with
#   an "@" in it (an email address) is not used.
#   Secret: no. Each reader can see the link.
PAYPAL_DONATE_LINK=

# =============================================================================
# Affiliate links (travel bookings)
# More: docs/settings.md#affiliate-links
# =============================================================================
# An affiliate link is a link to a booking website, such as a hotel or tour
# website, with your partner ID in it. When a reader clicks your link and
# books, the booking website pays you a commission (a part of the price).
# The reader pays the same price as without your link.
# With one or more links set below, the Weather page, the Events page and
# each event's own page show a "Plan a trip" box in the right-hand column
# (on a phone, after the page's main part). The box has one line for each
# link that you set: Hotels, Flights, Tours and activities, and Car rentals.
#   - Under the links, a short note tells readers that these are affiliate
#     links and that the site can earn a commission. The US Federal Trade
#     Commission (FTC) requires this note. The disclaimer on the Sources page
#     gets a paragraph about them too.
#   - Search engines are told that the links are paid links
#     (rel="sponsored"), as Google asks.
#   - The links are plain links: no script from the booking website, so the
#     pages stay as fast and as safe as before.
# Empty is OK for all four: then there is no "Plan a trip" box.
#
# How to get your links:
#   1. Choose an affiliate program that pays through PayPal (Stripe does not
#      work for Guam addresses). Two examples. Read their current terms on
#      their websites before you join, because terms change:
#        Travelpayouts (https://www.travelpayouts.com): one account for many
#          travel brands: hotels, flights, tours and car rentals. It pays to
#          PayPal once a month, when you have earned 50 US dollars or more.
#        Viator (https://partnerresources.viator.com): tours and activities.
#          It pays to PayPal every week.
#      The email address of your PayPal account must be the same as the one
#      that you sign up with, or the payments can fail.
#   2. Sign up. The program asks for your website (your domain, such as
#      https://guamheadlines.com) and how you will use the links. Approval
#      can take a few days.
#   3. In the program's dashboard, make a link to the page that readers must
#      see, for example the hotels in Guam or the tours in Guam. Programs
#      call this a "link generator", a "deep link" or a "tracking link".
#      Copy the full link. Your partner ID is in it.
#      Do not copy a banner code or a script (text that starts with "<"):
#      only a link that starts with https://. The site's security rules
#      block code that is pasted in.
#   4. Put each link on its line below. Leave empty the lines that you do
#      not use. Save the file.
#   5. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   6. Check it: open your domain followed by /weather. The "Plan a trip"
#      box is in the right-hand column. Click each line: the booking website
#      must open on the page that you chose, in a new tab. The program's
#      dashboard shows the clicks and the bookings, usually within a day.
#      If a line is missing, its link does not start with https:// (or
#      http://), or it has a space in it.

# AFFILIATE_HOTELS_LINK
#   Your affiliate link for hotels, such as a list of hotels in Guam on a
#   booking website. Shown as "Hotels".
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no Hotels line).
#   Example: https://www.example.com/hotels/guam?aid=123456
#   Secret: no. Each reader can see the link and the partner ID in it.
AFFILIATE_HOTELS_LINK=

# AFFILIATE_FLIGHTS_LINK
#   Your affiliate link for flights, such as a flight search to or from
#   Guam (GUM). Shown as "Flights".
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no Flights line).
#   Example: https://www.example.com/flights/GUM?marker=123456
#   Secret: no.
AFFILIATE_FLIGHTS_LINK=

# AFFILIATE_TOURS_LINK
#   Your affiliate link for tours and activities in Guam, such as boat
#   trips, diving and island tours. Shown as "Tours and activities".
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no Tours and activities line).
#   Example: https://www.example.com/guam/tours?pid=123456
#   Secret: no.
AFFILIATE_TOURS_LINK=

# AFFILIATE_CARS_LINK
#   Your affiliate link for car rentals in Guam. Shown as "Car rentals".
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no Car rentals line).
#   Example: https://www.example.com/car-rental/guam?affiliate=123456
#   Secret: no.
AFFILIATE_CARS_LINK=

# =============================================================================
# Visitor stats (private)
# More: docs/settings.md#visitor-stats
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines can count how many times each page is opened, and how many
# times readers follow a story's link to its news site (a "story click"). You
# see the totals on a private page of your site, /stats, after you type a
# password. It shows the top stories, the news sources and the topics that
# readers open most, and where readers come from: the website that each
# visit started from (such as google.com or facebook.com), or no website for
# a visit from a bookmark or the typed address. The counting happens on your
# own server:
#   - No script runs in the visitor's browser, so the pages stay as fast as
#     before. The PageSpeed scores do not change.
#   - No cookie is set, no outside service is used, no API key is needed,
#     and no IP address is stored. The site keeps only one number for each
#     page (or story, or website that sent a visit) for each day.
#   - Search engines, link previews and other robots are not counted.
# While the stats are on, each story link first opens a short address on
# your own site, such as https://guamheadlines.com/go/123. That address
# counts the click and sends the reader on to the story at once. Search
# engines are told not to follow these links.
# The disclaimer on the Sources page gets a short paragraph that tells
# visitors about the counts.
# Empty is OK: then nothing is counted, there is no /stats page, and the
# story links go straight to the news sites.
#
# How to turn the stats on:
#   1. Make a long random password. On the server (or in WSL), run:
#        openssl rand -hex 16
#      It shows 32 letters and digits, such as 3f9c2a7e5b1d4c8f9a0e6b2d7c4f1a3e
#      You can also make up a password of 16 or more letters and digits.
#   2. Put the password after STATS_PASSWORD= below, and save the file.
#   3. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   4. Open the stats page in a browser: your domain followed by /stats,
#      for example https://guamheadlines.com/stats
#      The browser asks for a user name and a password. Type any user name
#      (for example stats) and the password from step 1. The browser
#      remembers it until you close the browser.
#   The counts start at zero when you turn the stats on. A new view or click
#   can take up to a minute to show on the page.
# To turn the stats off, make the value empty again and run step 3. The
# counts stay in the database, and come back when you turn the stats on.
#
# Someone who types a wrong password 10 times within 15 minutes is blocked
# for an hour (even with the right password then), and you get a Discord
# alert. If that happens to you, wait an hour. A long, random password, as
# in step 1, can't be guessed. (scripts/harden-server.sh, see
# docs/security.md, replaces a password shorter than 12 characters with a
# long random one, and shows you the new one once. An empty one stays
# empty.)
#
# The same password also opens your other private pages, and the browser
# asks for it once for all of them:
#   - /admin/guides: write and change the visitor guides (/guides). Without
#     STATS_PASSWORD there is no editor, so no guide can be added. How to
#     write a guide: docs/guides.md.
#   - /admin/listings and /advertise/review, when paid listings or the
#     Advertise page are on (see those parts of this file).

# STATS_PASSWORD
#   The password of the private visitor stats page (/stats). A password here
#   also turns the counting on.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no counting and no stats page).
#   Example: 3f9c2a7e5b1d4c8f9a0e6b2d7c4f1a3e
#   Where to get it: you make it yourself (step 1 above).
#   Use only letters and digits. A "$" sign, or a space followed by "#",
#   changes the value (see HOW TO WRITE A SETTING at the top of this file).
#   Secret: yes. A person who has it can see your visitor numbers. Open the
#   stats page only over https (with SITE_DOMAIN set). Over plain http, the
#   password travels without protection.
STATS_PASSWORD=

# =============================================================================
# Newsletter feed (for SendFox or another email service)
# More: docs/newsletter.md
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines makes a feed of its top stories for an email newsletter. A
# feed (RSS) is a list of stories in a format that programs can read. An
# email service such as SendFox reads the feed and makes an email from the
# stories for your subscribers. The feed is at your domain followed by
# /feed.xml, for example https://guamheadlines.com/feed.xml
#   - On the days and at the time below (Guam time), the worker picks the
#     top stories that it found since the time before: the stories that
#     readers clicked most first (this needs the visitor stats above, else
#     the newest come first), at most 3 from one news site. This set of
#     stories is an "edition".
#   - The feed then shows the same edition until the next one. So the email
#     service sees new stories only just after an edition is made. Make the
#     editions about two hours before the emails go out, so the email
#     service has time to read the feed.
#   - Each story in the feed has its headline, photo, news site and the
#     first lines of its intro (not the full story), and a link to the
#     story. While the visitor stats are on, the links pass through
#     /go/<number>, so clicks in the emails show on the stats page too.
#   - No page of the site links to the feed, and search engines are told
#     not to list it.
# How to connect SendFox, step by step: docs/newsletter.md
# The defaults below make an edition every Tuesday and Friday at 10:00 in
# the morning, for emails that go out at noon.

# NEWSLETTER_DAYS
#   The days of the week when the worker makes a new edition. Write the day
#   names in English, or their first three letters, with a comma between
#   them.
#   Required: no. Default: tue,fri
#   Example: mon,wed,fri
#   Empty: no feed (the /feed.xml address shows "Not found").
NEWSLETTER_DAYS=tue,fri

# NEWSLETTER_TIME
#   The time of day when the worker makes the edition, in Guam time (ChST).
#   Write it on a 24-hour clock: 10:00 is 10 in the morning, and 14:30 is
#   half past 2 in the afternoon. Make it about two hours before your email
#   service sends the email.
#   Required: no. Default: 10:00
NEWSLETTER_TIME=10:00

# NEWSLETTER_STORIES
#   The number of stories in each edition.
#   Required: no. Default: 12. Minimum: 1. Maximum: 50.
NEWSLETTER_STORIES=12

# --- The sign-up box (SendFox) ---
# With a SendFox API token (a password that SendFox gives to a program),
# every page gets a small "Newsletter" box in the right-hand column, where
# readers type their email address to join your SendFox list. The site also
# gets a newsletter page (/newsletter) with the same form, and the footer of
# every page links to it.
#   - The form is part of the site itself. The reader's browser sends the
#     address to your site, and your server passes it on to SendFox. No
#     SendFox script loads in the reader's browser, so the pages stay as
#     fast and as safe as before (the PageSpeed scores do not change).
#   - Robots that fill in forms are kept out with a field that people do
#     not see, and with a limit: at most 5 sign-ups an hour from one
#     internet address.
#   - SendFox keeps one contact for each address, so a reader who signs up
#     twice gets the newsletter once.
#   - The disclaimer on the Sources page gets a short paragraph that says
#     where the addresses go.
# Empty is OK: then there is no sign-up box and no newsletter page.
#
# How to turn the sign-up box on:
#   1. Sign in to SendFox (https://sendfox.com). Open the page for API
#      tokens: https://sendfox.com/account/oauth
#      (In the menus: your account settings, at your name or picture at the
#      top right, then "Connections" or "Integrations", then "API" or
#      "Personal Access Tokens". SendFox changes the names now and then.)
#   2. Make a new token, and name it GU Headlines. SendFox shows the token
#      only once, so copy it at once. It is a long line of letters, digits
#      and dots.
#   3. Put the token after SENDFOX_API_TOKEN= below, and save the file.
#   4. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   5. Check it:
#        docker compose exec web guheadlines sendfox-check
#      It shows your SendFox account, its lists, and the list that
#      sign-ups join. If your account has more than one list, it says
#      "Problem" and names them: copy the name of the list that new readers
#      should join after SENDFOX_LIST= below, and do steps 4 and 5 again.
#   6. Sign up on your own site, in the Newsletter box, with an email
#      address of yours. The page says "Thanks, you're signed up". In
#      SendFox, the address shows in your contacts within a minute.
#      SendFox then sends that address an email to confirm it, and the site
#      tells each reader to look for it in their inbox. Open it and confirm.
#      Only confirmed addresses get the newsletter. If SendFox shows the
#      address as "Unconfirmed" and no such email comes, see "If sign-ups
#      stay unconfirmed" in docs/newsletter.md.
# To turn the box off, make SENDFOX_API_TOKEN empty and run step 4.

# SENDFOX_API_TOKEN
#   Your SendFox API token (steps 1 to 3 above). A token here also turns the
#   sign-up box on.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no sign-up box).
#   Example: a long line of letters, digits and dots, often starting with
#   eyJ. Paste all of it, on one line.
#   Secret: yes. A person who has it can see and change your SendFox
#   contacts and lists. If someone else sees it, delete the token in SendFox
#   (on the same page) and make a new one.
SENDFOX_API_TOKEN=

# SENDFOX_LIST
#   The SendFox list that new readers join: its name, as SendFox shows it,
#   or its number (sendfox-check in step 5 shows both). Capitals do not
#   matter.
#   Required: no. Default: empty, which means the only list of your account.
#   With more than one list, empty does not work: name the list here.
#   Example: Guam Headlines
#   Secret: no.
SENDFOX_LIST=

# =============================================================================
# PayPal (taking payments on the site)
# More: docs/settings.md#paypal
# =============================================================================
# The parts of the site that sell something (for example the paid listings
# below) take the money through PayPal. The buyer pays on PayPal's own page,
# with a PayPal account or with a card as a guest. Then PayPal sends the
# buyer back to your site. The money goes into your PayPal account. From
# there, you can move it to your bank account.
#   - The site shows no PayPal button and loads no PayPal script. A plain
#     link opens PayPal's page, so your pages stay as fast and as safe as
#     before (the PageSpeed scores do not change).
#   - Your server talks to PayPal with two values from PayPal: a "Client ID"
#     (like a user name) and a "Secret" (like a password).
#   - PayPal has two separate systems. "Sandbox" is for tests: the payments
#     use pretend money and test accounts that PayPal makes for you. "Live"
#     takes real money. Each system has its own Client ID and Secret.
#   - PayPal keeps a fee from each payment. PayPal shows its fees on its
#     "Fees" page (the link is at the bottom of paypal.com).
#   - No email address of yours is shown on the site. PayPal sends each
#     buyer a receipt by itself.
# Empty is OK: then nothing on the site takes payments.
#
# How to connect PayPal, step by step:
#   1. You need a PayPal Business account. If you have a personal PayPal
#      account, you can change it to a Business account for free: sign in at
#      https://www.paypal.com, open Settings (the gear at the top right),
#      and click "Upgrade to a Business account". Or make a new Business
#      account at https://www.paypal.com/business
#   2. Open https://developer.paypal.com in your browser, and sign in with
#      the same email address and password as your PayPal Business account.
#      (This is PayPal's site for programs that use PayPal. Signing in here
#      costs nothing.)
#   3. Click "Apps & Credentials" in the top menu. At the top of that page
#      there is a switch with two sides: "Sandbox" and "Live". Make sure
#      that "Sandbox" is on. Start with Sandbox, so that you can test with
#      pretend money first.
#   4. Click "Create App". For "App Name", type GU Headlines. For "Type",
#      pick "Merchant". Click "Create App".
#      (PayPal may already show an app named "Default Application". You can
#      use that one instead of making a new one.)
#   5. The page of the app shows the "Client ID" (a long line of letters and
#      digits) and, under "Secret key 1", a hidden Secret: click "Show" to
#      see it. Copy the Client ID after PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID= below, and the
#      Secret after PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET= below. Leave PAYPAL_MODE=sandbox.
#      Save the file.
#   6. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   7. Check it:
#        docker compose exec web guheadlines paypal-check
#      It says "OK" when PayPal accepts the Client ID and the Secret. If it
#      says "Problem", it says why.
#   8. Make a test payment with a pretend buyer. PayPal made a test buyer
#      for you: on developer.paypal.com, click "Testing Tools", then
#      "Sandbox Accounts". The account whose type is "Personal" is the test
#      buyer (its email address ends in @personal.example.com). Click the
#      three dots beside it, then "View/Edit account", to see its password.
#      Buy something on your own site (for example a classified ad), and
#      sign in on PayPal's test page with that test buyer. No real money
#      moves. To see the payment, sign in at https://www.sandbox.paypal.com
#      with the test account whose type is "Business".
#   9. When the test works, take real payments: on "Apps & Credentials",
#      move the switch to "Live", and do steps 4 and 5 again in Live (the
#      Live app has its own Client ID and Secret). Put PAYPAL_MODE=live
#      below, and do steps 6 and 7 again. PayPal can ask you to confirm your
#      business details and bank account before it lets you take live
#      payments. Follow its steps on paypal.com.
# To stop taking payments, make PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID empty and run step 6.

# PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID
#   The Client ID of your PayPal app (step 5 above).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no payments).
#   Example: AeA1QIZXiflr1_-r0U2UbWTziOWX1GRQer5jkUq4ZfWT5qwb6qQRPq7jDtv57TL4POEEezGLdutcxnkJ
#   Secret: no. Buyers never see it, but it is not a password either.
PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=

# PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET
#   The Secret of your PayPal app (step 5 above).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no payments).
#   Example: a long line of letters, digits, dashes and underscores, like
#   the Client ID.
#   Secret: yes. A person who has it can use PayPal as your site, for
#   example to give money back from your account. If someone else sees it,
#   open your app on developer.paypal.com, click "Add new secret", put the
#   new Secret here, and then delete the old one.
PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET=

# PAYPAL_MODE
#   Which PayPal the Client ID and the Secret are from:
#     sandbox   PayPal's test system (pretend money). Use it first.
#     live      Real payments.
#   A Sandbox Client ID does not work with live, and a Live Client ID does
#   not work with sandbox: paypal-check (step 7) then says "Problem".
#   Required: no. Default: sandbox.
PAYPAL_MODE=sandbox

# =============================================================================
# Paid listings (classifieds, featured jobs and events, business directory)
# More: docs/settings.md#paid-listings
# =============================================================================
# People and businesses can pay to put a listing on the site:
#   - Classified ads on a new Classifieds page: things for sale, cars and
#     trucks, housing, services and wanted ads.
#   - Featured job posts, pinned at the top of the Jobs page.
#   - Featured events, at the top of the Events page, in gold on the month
#     calendar, and on the home page until the event is over.
#   - A Business directory on a new Directory page. A basic entry (name,
#     category, village, phone and a few lines) is free. The featured
#     upgrade adds a photo and a link to the business's website, and puts
#     the entry at the top of the directory.
# How it works:
#   1. The person fills in a form on the site (from a button at the top of
#      each of these pages). Nobody needs an account or a password.
#   2. The site shows them a private page with their listing, and a "Pay
#      with PayPal" link (see PayPal above). A listing that is never paid
#      for is deleted after a week.
#   3. You check each listing before it shows (free basic entries too).
#      Open your domain followed by /admin/listings, for example
#      https://guamheadlines.com/admin/listings
#      The browser asks for a user name and a password. Type any user name
#      and your STATS_PASSWORD (see Visitor stats above). The /stats page
#      also links to it, with the number of listings that wait.
#      "Approve" puts the listing on the site at once. "Reject" gives the
#      money back through PayPal, with your reason on the person's page.
#   4. With DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL set (see Alerts in Discord, further down),
#      Discord tells you each time a listing waits for review.
# The listings need both PayPal (PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID and PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET)
# and STATS_PASSWORD. Without them, the Classifieds and Directory pages and
# the "Post" buttons do not show.
# Prices are in US dollars. Write only the number, such as 5 or 12.50. Do
# not write the "$" sign: Docker Compose changes a value with "$" in it (see
# HOW TO WRITE A SETTING at the top of this file). A price of 0 turns that
# kind of listing off (for the directory: the whole directory).
# A new price is used for listings sent after you apply it with:
#   docker compose up -d
# Listings sent earlier keep the price that they were sent with.

# CLASSIFIED_PRICE
#   What a classified ad costs, in US dollars.
#   Required: no. Default: 5. Use 0 to have no Classifieds page.
CLASSIFIED_PRICE=5

# CLASSIFIED_DAYS
#   How many days a classified ad shows, counted from when you approve it.
#   Required: no. Default: 30. From 1 to 365.
CLASSIFIED_DAYS=30

# FEATURED_JOB_PRICE
#   What a featured job post costs, in US dollars. The job boards' own
#   postings on the Jobs page stay free; this is only for an employer who
#   wants a post pinned at the top.
#   Required: no. Default: 25. Use 0 to offer no featured job posts.
FEATURED_JOB_PRICE=25

# FEATURED_JOB_DAYS
#   How many days a featured job post stays at the top of the Jobs page,
#   counted from when you approve it.
#   Required: no. Default: 7. From 1 to 365.
FEATURED_JOB_DAYS=7

# FEATURED_EVENT_PRICE
#   What a featured event costs, in US dollars. A featured event shows until
#   it is over, so it has no number of days. The event must start within 90
#   days and last at most 14 days.
#   Required: no. Default: 15. Use 0 to offer no featured events.
FEATURED_EVENT_PRICE=15

# BUSINESS_UPGRADE_PRICE
#   What the featured upgrade of a directory entry costs, in US dollars.
#   Basic entries are always free.
#   Required: no. Default: 50. Use 0 to have no Directory page.
BUSINESS_UPGRADE_PRICE=50

# BUSINESS_UPGRADE_DAYS
#   How many days the featured upgrade lasts, counted from when you approve
#   it. Then the entry stays in the directory as a basic entry, without the
#   photo and the link.
#   Required: no. Default: 365 (a year). From 1 to 3650.
BUSINESS_UPGRADE_DAYS=365

# =============================================================================
# Advertise page (businesses book and pay for ads themselves)
# More: docs/settings.md#advertise-page
# =============================================================================
# The Advertise page (/advertise) lets a local business buy an ad without
# writing to you first. It shows your real numbers from the visitor stats
# (page views in the last 30 days, clicks on the sponsor spot and on the
# newsletter's stories), what is for sale, and a booking form. Two things can
# be sold, each with its own price below:
#   - The sponsor spot, by the week (Monday to Sunday, Guam time). In a week
#     that a business booked, its picture takes the place of SPONSOR_IMAGE
#     (above). In the other weeks, SPONSOR_IMAGE shows as before, or, with no
#     SPONSOR_IMAGE, a small "Your business here" box that links to the
#     Advertise page.
#   - A sponsor line in the newsletter, by the edition: one or two
#     sentences with a link, first in the email and marked "Sponsor".
# How a booking goes:
#   1. The business picks its weeks or editions, fills in its name, email
#      address and website, and adds its picture (sponsor spot) or its words
#      (newsletter). Its dates are held for it for an hour.
#   2. It pays on PayPal's page (a PayPal account or a card). The money goes
#      to your PayPal account straight away.
#   3. You get a Discord alert (with DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL set). The ad does NOT
#      show yet: you look at it first on /advertise/review and press
#      "Approve", or "Turn down and refund" (PayPal then gives all of the
#      money back to the business by itself).
#   4. The business has its own page for its booking, with a secret address.
#      It shows where the booking stands, and later how many page views and
#      clicks its ad got. No logins: that address is its way back.
# The page is on only when all of these are set:
#   - PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID and PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET (the PayPal part above);
#   - STATS_PASSWORD (the Visitor stats part of this file): it opens the
#     review page, the same way it opens the stats page;
#   - at least one price below. With no price, there is no Advertise page.
# The page adds no script and loads nothing from PayPal, so the PageSpeed
# scores stay the same. Your own email address is never shown.
#
# How to start selling ads, step by step:
#   1. Do the PayPal steps above (start with PAYPAL_MODE=sandbox), and set
#      STATS_PASSWORD if you have not yet.
#   2. Choose your prices, and put them below. Start low while the site is
#      new; you can change them any time, and ads that are already booked
#      keep the price they were booked at.
#   3. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   4. Check it: open https://your-site/advertise (with your own address).
#      The page shows your numbers and a form for each thing that has a
#      price. A link "Advertise" is at the bottom of every page.
#   5. Make a test booking: fill in the form and pay with PayPal's test
#      buyer (step 8 of the PayPal part). Then open
#      https://your-site/advertise/review, sign in with any user name and
#      your STATS_PASSWORD, and press "Approve" or "Turn down and refund".
#   6. When the test works, switch PayPal to live (step 9 of the PayPal
#      part).
# Look at /advertise/review when an alert comes, or once a day if you have
# no Discord alerts: a paid ad waits there until you approve it. Approve it
# before its first day, or it starts late.
# To stop selling, make both prices empty and run step 3. Ads that are
# already approved still show in their weeks.

# SPONSOR_WEEK_PRICE
#   The price of the sponsor spot for one week, in US dollars.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (the sponsor spot is not sold online).
#   Example: 25   (or 25.50 for dollars and cents)
#   Do not use a "$" sign (see HOW TO WRITE A SETTING at the top of this
#   file). Up to 10000.
#   Secret: no.
SPONSOR_WEEK_PRICE=

# NEWSLETTER_SPONSOR_PRICE
#   The price of the sponsor line in one newsletter edition, in US dollars.
#   Only with the newsletter feed on (NEWSLETTER_DAYS).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (the newsletter line is not sold).
#   Example: 15
#   Do not use a "$" sign. Up to 10000.
#   Secret: no.
NEWSLETTER_SPONSOR_PRICE=

# =============================================================================
# Your own email server (addresses at your domain, webmail, the site's email)
# More: docs/email-server.md
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines can run its own mail server next to the site, in two more
# containers:
#   mailserver   Receives mail for your addresses (like you@guamheadlines.com),
#                sends mail (yours, and the site's emails such as the bid
#                alerts), keeps the mailboxes and filters spam.
#   webmail      Read and write your mail in the browser (Roundcube), at
#                https://mail.guamheadlines.com. Mail apps on your phone and
#                computer work too.
# Both stay off until you set the two settings below. Together they use
# about 350 MB of memory.
#
# A mail server needs more than these settings. Other mail servers trust
# your mail only with: a DNS record for the mail server's name, a few DNS
# records that prove the mail is really yours (MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC), and
# "reverse DNS" from your server company. Without them, your emails land in
# spam, or are refused. docs/email-server.md walks you through all of it,
# and ./scripts/mail.sh does most of the work.
#
# In short (the guide has each step in detail):
#   1. Run this on the server. It tells you what is missing:
#        ./scripts/mail.sh check
#   2. In the DNS of your domain, add an A record with the name "mail" and
#      the IP address of your server as the value (like the one for the site).
#   3. Ask your server company to open outgoing port 25, and to set the
#      reverse DNS of the server's IP address to mail.guamheadlines.com.
#   4. Set COMPOSE_PROFILES and MAIL_HOSTNAME below. Save the file.
#   5. Run this. It makes your mailbox and the site's mailbox, starts the
#      mail server, and lists the DNS records to add:
#        ./scripts/mail.sh setup
#   6. Put the five lines that it prints into the "Sending email" part below,
#      and run: docker compose up -d
#   7. Add the DNS records, then send a test email (the guide tells how).

# COMPOSE_PROFILES
#   Which extra parts Docker Compose starts. "mail" starts the mail server
#   and the webmail. (A "profile" is a group of containers that start only
#   when you ask for them.)
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no mail server and no webmail).
#   Example: mail
#   To turn the mail server off again: empty this setting, then run
#     docker compose --profile mail rm -sf mailserver webmail
#     docker compose up -d
#   Your mail stays saved (in Docker volumes) for when you turn it on again.
#   Only Docker Compose reads this setting.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# MAIL_HOSTNAME
#   The name of your mail server: "mail." and your domain. Your addresses
#   are name@<your domain>, such as you@guamheadlines.com, and the webmail is
#   at https://<this name>. caddy gets a certificate for this name, and the
#   mail server uses it too.
#   Required: only with COMPOSE_PROFILES=mail. Default: empty.
#   Example: mail.guamheadlines.com
#   The name must point at this server before you turn the mail server on
#   (step 2 above). Choose it once: the MX record, the reverse DNS and the
#   settings in mail apps all use it.
#   Secret: no.
MAIL_HOSTNAME=

# =============================================================================
# Sending email (for bid alerts and renewal reminders)
# More: docs/bid-alerts.md#1-set-up-sending-email
# =============================================================================
# The site sends email for the bid alerts below (the alerts, the welcome
# email, and the link to a subscriber's page), and the renewal reminders
# (one email to a buyer whose paid listing or ad ends soon, see "Renewal
# reminders" further down). It hands each email to a mail server, which
# delivers it. This is not SendFox: SendFox sends the
# newsletter to a whole list, but it cannot send one email to one person.
#   - Programs hand email to a mail server through "SMTP", the standard way
#     to send email. The five settings below tell the site which mail
#     server to use: its name (SMTP_HOST), its port (SMTP_PORT), the user
#     name and password that it asks for, and the sender (MAIL_FROM).
#   - The mail server can run on your own server, or belong to an email
#     company. Its own setup steps give you the values to put below. With
#     your own email server (the part above), "./scripts/mail.sh setup"
#     makes the site's mailbox and prints all five values.
#   - A mail server sends from a domain that you own. It needs a few lines
#     (DNS records, often named SPF, DKIM and DMARC) where you manage the
#     DNS of your domain. They prove that the emails really come from you,
#     and keep them out of spam folders.
#   - The sender address (MAIL_FROM) shows only in the emails, never on the
#     site. Nobody needs to read its mailbox: the emails tell subscribers to
#     use the link to their own page.
# Empty is OK: then the site sends no email, bid alerts stay off, and no
# renewal reminders go out.
#
# When the mail server is ready, step by step:
#   1. Put its values in SMTP_HOST, SMTP_PORT, SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASSWORD
#      below, and the sender in MAIL_FROM. Save the file.
#   2. Apply it:
#        docker compose up -d
#   3. Send a test email to an address of yours:
#        docker compose exec web guheadlines mail-test you@example.com
#      It says "Test email sent" when the mail server took it. Look in that
#      inbox, and in its spam folder. If it says "Problem", it says why. If
#      it waits a long time and then fails, the site cannot reach the mail
#      server: check SMTP_HOST and SMTP_PORT, then do steps 2 and 3 again.

# SMTP_HOST
#   The name of the mail server. The site always encrypts the connection,
#   so the mail server needs a certificate (like the one for https) for
#   this name.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no email).
#   Example: mail.guamheadlines.com
#   Secret: no.
SMTP_HOST=

# SMTP_PORT
#   The port of the mail server (a port is like a door number on a server).
#   465 uses an encrypted connection from the start. Other ports (587 is
#   the usual one) start plain and then switch to encryption. Either way,
#   the password and the emails go encrypted.
#   Required: no. Default: 587
#   Example: 587
SMTP_PORT=587

# SMTP_USER
#   The user name that the mail server asks for. With your own email server,
#   it is the whole address of the site's mailbox.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (the mail server asks for no sign-in).
#   Example: alerts@guamheadlines.com
#   Secret: no.
SMTP_USER=

# SMTP_PASSWORD
#   The password that goes with SMTP_USER.
#   Required: no. Default: empty.
#   Secret: yes. A person who has it can send email as your domain. If
#   someone else sees it, change it on the mail server, and put the new
#   one here.
SMTP_PASSWORD=

# MAIL_FROM
#   The sender of the emails: a name, and an address at your domain between
#   < and >. The part before the @ can be any word, such as alerts: the
#   address does not need a mailbox. With your own email server, it must be
#   the address in SMTP_USER: the mail server sends only as the mailbox that
#   signs in. Replies and bounced emails then land in that mailbox.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no email).
#   Example: GU Headlines <alerts@guamheadlines.com>
#   Secret: no. It shows in the emails only, never on the site.
MAIL_FROM=

# =============================================================================
# Bid alerts (paid email alerts for new bids)
# More: docs/bid-alerts.md
# =============================================================================
# Businesses pay a monthly price through PayPal to get an email when a new
# bid on the Bids page matches their trade (construction, IT, medical and
# more) or their own words. The page for it is /bids/alerts, for example
# https://guamheadlines.com/bids/alerts. The Bids page, the footer and the
# Sources page link to it.
#   - Payment: PayPal takes the price every month until the subscriber
#     cancels. The site makes the PayPal "plan" (the monthly price) by
#     itself, the first time that someone signs up.
#   - No login or password: every email has a link to the subscriber's own
#     page, where they change their trades and words, or cancel. A lost link
#     is sent again to the address, from the bid alerts page.
#   - After each bids check (BIDS_INTERVAL_HOURS), each subscriber gets one
#     email with the new bids that match, if there are any. A bid is never
#     sent twice.
#   - When a payment fails, PayPal tries again, and the alerts pause after
#     two failures. When a subscriber cancels, the alerts go on until the end
#     of the month that they paid for.
#   - New subscribers, failed payments and cancels are posted in your
#     Discord channel when DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL is set (no email addresses).
#   - To see the subscribers:
#       docker compose exec web guheadlines bid-alerts
# Bid alerts need all of these: BID_ALERTS_PRICE below, PayPal (the
# PayPal part above), email (the "Sending email" part above), and
# SITE_DOMAIN or SITE_URL (for the links in the emails). Until all are set,
# there is no bid alerts page. The bid-alerts command says what is missing.
#
# How to turn bid alerts on:
#   1. Set up PayPal (above) with PAYPAL_MODE=sandbox, and email (above).
#   2. Put a price below, for example BID_ALERTS_PRICE=15, and run:
#        docker compose up -d
#        docker compose exec web guheadlines bid-alerts
#      It says "Bid alerts are on" and shows the sign-up page.
#   3. Test it with the pretend buyer of the PayPal sandbox (see step 8 of
#      the PayPal part): sign up on your bid alerts page with your own email
#      address, and pay on PayPal's test page. You come back to your page,
#      and a welcome email comes. Then click "Cancel my subscription".
#   4. Take real payments: change PayPal to live (step 9 of the PayPal
#      part), and run docker compose up -d. The site makes a new plan in
#      live PayPal by itself.

# BID_ALERTS_PRICE
#   The monthly price of bid alerts, in US dollars. Write the number only,
#   such as 15 or 9.99. To change the price later, change it here: new
#   subscribers pay the new price, and subscribers who joined before keep
#   the price that they joined at. Up to 10000.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no bid alerts).
#   Example: 15
#   Secret: no.
BID_ALERTS_PRICE=

# =============================================================================
# Renewal reminders (an email before a paid listing or ad ends)
# More: docs/settings.md#renewal-reminders
# =============================================================================
# Paid listings and booked ads end by themselves on their last day: a
# classified ad after CLASSIFIED_DAYS, a featured job post after
# FEATURED_JOB_DAYS, a business's featured upgrade after
# BUSINESS_UPGRADE_DAYS (see "Paid listings" above), and a booked ad after
# its last week or newsletter edition (see "Advertise page" above). You do
# not need to do anything: the pages stop showing them on that date. (A
# featured event shows until the event is over, so it has no reminder.)
#
# A few days before the end, the site sends the buyer ONE email, so that
# they can pay for more time if they want to. The email has a link to their
# own page (no login, the same private page as before):
#   - A listing: the page has a "Renew" button. PayPal takes the same price
#     again (the price in this file at that moment), and the listing shows
#     for the same number of days more, added to its end date. It does NOT
#     come back to you for review, because nothing in it changed. With
#     DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL set, Discord tells you about each renewal. The
#     email goes to the email address that the buyer paid with on PayPal.
#   - A booked ad: the page has a "Run this ad again" form, with the next
#     free weeks or editions already ticked. It makes a new booking with
#     the same picture or words, which is paid and approved like any other
#     booking (you still press "Approve" on /advertise/review). The email
#     goes to the email address in the booking. No email goes out when the
#     business has already booked a later week or edition.
# Renewal reminders need email (the "Sending email" part above) and
# SITE_DOMAIN or SITE_URL (for the link in the email). Until both are set,
# no reminder is sent, but listings and ads still end on time, and buyers
# can still renew from their own page.
# The worker checks after each news check (SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES, every
# hour unless you changed it). Each listing or ad gets one email for each
# end date; after a renewal, the new end date gets its own email.
#
# How to check that it works (with PayPal in sandbox mode):
#   1. Set up email (above) and send yourself a test email with mail-test.
#   2. Put CLASSIFIED_DAYS=1 for the test, and run: docker compose up -d
#   3. Post a classified ad, pay with PayPal's test buyer (whose email
#      address you can see in the PayPal developer dashboard, under
#      Testing Tools, then Sandbox Accounts), and approve it on
#      /admin/listings.
#   4. Within an hour, the worker sends the email "Your classified ad ends
#      on ..." to the test buyer's address. Check that it went:
#        docker compose logs worker | grep "renewal reminders"
#      It says "renewal reminders: 1 emails sent". (PayPal's test buyer
#      has a made-up address, so the email itself reaches nobody.)
#   5. Open the ad's own page: it has a "Renew for $5 with PayPal" button.
#      Pay with the test buyer again, and the end date moves 1 day later.
#   6. Put CLASSIFIED_DAYS back to 30 and run docker compose up -d again.

# RENEWAL_REMINDER_DAYS
#   How many days before a paid listing or a booked ad ends the buyer gets
#   the email. 3 means the email goes out 3 days before the last day.
#   Use 0 to send no reminders (listings and ads still end on time, and
#   buyers can still renew from their own page).
#   Required: no. Default: 3. From 0 to 30.
#   Example: 3
#   Secret: no.
RENEWAL_REMINDER_DAYS=3

# =============================================================================
# Checking for news
# More: docs/settings.md#checking-for-news
# =============================================================================
# The worker visits each news site in config/sources.yaml on a schedule. It
# saves the new stories and their photos.

# SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker checks all the news sites, in minutes. The checks
#   follow the clock: 60 means at the start of each hour, 30 means at :00
#   and :30, and 120 means every second hour.
#   Required: no. Default: 60. Minimum: 5.
#   Use a number that divides an hour evenly (15, 20 or 30) or a number of
#   whole hours (60, 120 or 180). Checks more often than every 30 minutes
#   rarely find more news, and they send more requests to the news sites.
SCRAPE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60

# SCRAPE_ON_START
#   true: check the news sites and the job boards immediately each time the
#   worker starts.
#   false: wait for the next scheduled check.
#   Required: no. Default: true.
SCRAPE_ON_START=true

# MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE
#   The maximum number of new stories that the worker reads from one site in
#   one check. A busy site, or the first check, can have more. The worker
#   gets the rest in catch-up checks (see CATCH_UP_SECONDS). This limit
#   prevents too many requests to one site at the same time.
#   Required: no. Default: 40. Minimum: 1.
MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE=40

# CATCH_UP_SECONDS
#   Sometimes the worker cannot read all of a site in one check. The site
#   had more than MAX_NEW_PER_SOURCE new stories, or it answered "too many
#   requests". Then the worker comes back for only that site after this many
#   seconds. It comes back until the site is done, and then it goes back to
#   the regular schedule. If a site asks for a longer pause, the worker waits
#   that long, up to 10 minutes.
#   Required: no. Default: 60. Minimum: 30.
CATCH_UP_SECONDS=60

# JOBS_INTERVAL_HOURS
#   How often the worker reads the job boards for the Jobs page, in hours.
#   The list of job boards is in config/jobs.yaml. The worker reads them at
#   its first check after it starts, and then once in each period of this
#   many hours.
#   Required: no. Default: 6. Minimum: 1.
#   More: docs/jobs.md
JOBS_INTERVAL_HOURS=6

# EVENTS_INTERVAL_HOURS
#   How often the worker reads the event calendars for the Events page, in
#   hours. The list of calendars is in config/events.yaml. The worker reads
#   them at its first check after it starts, and then once in each period of
#   this many hours. Each check keeps the events of the next 90 days.
#   Required: no. Default: 3. Minimum: 1.
#   More: docs/events.md
EVENTS_INTERVAL_HOURS=3

# BIDS_INTERVAL_HOURS
#   How often the worker reads the government bid lists for the Bids page,
#   in hours. A "bid" here is a government agency asking businesses to offer
#   a price for goods, services or construction work: an Invitation for Bid
#   (IFB) or a Request for Proposals (RFP). The list of places it reads is in
#   config/bids.yaml. The worker reads them at its first check after it
#   starts, and then once in each period of this many hours. Bids leave the
#   page by themselves once their due date has passed.
#   Agencies post a few new bids a week, so every 6 hours is plenty. A
#   smaller number only means more visits to the government websites.
#   Required: no. Default: 6. Minimum: 1.
#   More: docs/bids.md
BIDS_INTERVAL_HOURS=6

# =============================================================================
# Weather, typhoon warnings and tides
# More: docs/settings.md#weather
# =============================================================================
# The worker also reads the weather and the tides for Guam from three
# services of the United States government. All are free. They need no key
# and no sign-up.
#   - The National Weather Service (NWS) office in Tiyan: the watches,
#     warnings and advisories for Guam and its coastal waters, the forecast
#     (for the week and for each hour), the latest reading at Guam
#     International Airport, the surf forecast (rip currents, surf height,
#     water temperature) and the coastal waters forecast (wind and seas, for
#     boating).
#   - The Joint Typhoon Warning Center (JTWC): the tropical storms and
#     typhoons in the western North Pacific.
#   - NOAA Tides & Currents: the times and heights of the high and low tides
#     (see TIDE_STATION below).
# The site shows the weather in three places:
#   - A banner at the top of every page while NWS has a watch or a warning
#     for Guam, for example a Typhoon Warning. A warning is red and a watch is
#     amber. A visitor can open the banner to read the full NWS text.
#   - A weather box at the top of the home page: the temperature, the
#     forecast, the storm list, today's high and low tides and the
#     advisories (for example a Small Craft Advisory).
#   - The Weather page (the "Weather" link in the top bar): all of the above
#     in more detail, the next 24 hours, the week, the surf and the sea, and
#     the tides for the day, the week or the month. Each part links to the
#     service that it comes from.
# If the worker cannot reach a service, the site keeps the last weather that
# it got. A warning stays in the banner until its end time.

# WEATHER_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker reads the weather, in minutes. A new warning shows
#   on the site at most this many minutes after NWS sends it, plus a few
#   minutes for the site and the browsers to show the new page.
#   0: do not read the weather. The site shows no banner, no weather box and
#   no Weather page.
#   Required: no. Default: 10. Minimum: 5 (or 0 to turn it off).
#   How to check that it works:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines weather
#   It reads the weather and the tides now and shows one line for each part
#   (alerts, conditions, forecast, hourly, surf, marine, storms and tides):
#   the data that it saved, or "ERROR" and the reason.
WEATHER_INTERVAL_MINUTES=10

# TIDE_STATION
#   Which NOAA tide station the tide times come from. A tide station is a
#   place where NOAA measures or works out the tides; each one has a number,
#   its "station ID". Tide times are a little different around the island,
#   so you could pick the station nearest to the beaches your visitors care
#   about. The default, 1630000, is Apra Harbor, which most Guam tide tables
#   use.
#   Tide times are worked out years ahead, so the worker reads the next month
#   only every 6 hours (and at once when you change this setting).
#   Empty (TIDE_STATION=): no tides on the site. The rest of the weather
#   stays.
#   Required: no. Default: 1630000 (Apra Harbor).
#   To find another station's ID:
#     1. Open https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/tide_predictions.html in a
#        web browser.
#     2. Find Guam on that page: in its list of states and territories, or
#        on its map.
#     3. Each station is shown with its name and its ID, a number like
#        1630000. Copy the ID.
#     4. Put it here, for example: TIDE_STATION=1630000
#     5. Restart the worker and the website (docker compose up -d), then
#        check it with: docker compose exec worker guheadlines weather
#        The "tides" line shows the station's name and the tides, or ERROR
#        and the reason (for example a wrong ID).
#   Anything other than letters and digits turns the tides off.
TIDE_STATION=1630000

# =============================================================================
# Power and water outages, and gas prices
# More: docs/settings.md#outages-and-gas-prices
# =============================================================================
# The Outages page (the "Outages" link in the top bar) lists the power and
# water outages that the island's utilities announce, by day (today, coming
# up, and the last 3 days), and the boil-water notices of the last 30 days.
# They come from three public pages. None needs a key or a sign-up:
#   - Power: the Guam Power Authority's (GPA) outage notices on GuamALERTS,
#     the island's official alert service (web.guamalerts.com), which GPA's
#     own website links to.
#   - Water: the Guam Waterworks Authority's (GWA) outage updates, the page
#     that GWA's website calls "Current Outages".
#   - Boil-water notices: from GWA, and from the Guam Environmental
#     Protection Agency (Guam EPA) news.
# Outages are only on that page: not in the warning banner and not on the
# home page. Each notice links to the agency's own notice. If the worker
# cannot reach a page, the site keeps the notices that it already has and
# says when it last reached that agency, with a link to its page.

# OUTAGES_INTERVAL_MINUTES
#   How often the worker reads the outage notices, in minutes. A new outage
#   shows on the site at most this many minutes after the agency posts it,
#   plus a few minutes for the site and the browsers to show the new page.
#   0: do not read them. The site has no Outages page and no "Outages" link.
#   Required: no. Default: 15. Minimum: 5 (or 0 to turn it off).
#   How to check that it works:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines outages
#   It reads the three pages now and shows, for each, how many notices it
#   found and the first few titles, or "ERROR" and the reason.
OUTAGES_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15

# GAS_PRICE_DAYS
#   The sidebar (the column beside the stories, on every page) shows Guam's
#   gas prices: regular, premium and diesel, for one gallon. Prices are the
#   same at every station on the island. No agency or company publishes them
#   in a form a program may read, so the site reads them from the local news
#   stories that it already collects ("Gas prices up again: $5.94 a gallon of
#   regular"). Each price shows the day of its story and links to it.
#   This setting is how many days back the site looks for such a story. A
#   price from an older story is not shown, because it may be out of date.
#   When no story in that time gives a price, the sidebar shows no gas
#   prices at all (nothing is made up).
#   Because the prices come from the news, they change on the site when the
#   news reports a change, usually the same day that the stations change
#   them.
#   0: no gas prices on the site.
#   Required: no. Default: 45. Maximum: 365.
GAS_PRICE_DAYS=45

# =============================================================================
# Sources that need a key
# =============================================================================
# Two sources need a free API key: the USAJOBS job board (Jobs page) and the
# Guam Legislature's calendar (Events page). (An API is a way for programs to
# get data from a service. The key tells the service which program asks.)
# Without its key, GU Headlines skips that source and shows the reason on the
# site. All other sources work without a key. Empty is OK for all three values.

# --- USAJOBS key (federal jobs on the Jobs page) ---
#   How to get the key:
#     1. Open https://developer.usajobs.gov/apirequest/ and fill in the
#        form. Use an email address that you can read.
#     2. Wait for the email from USAJOBS. It contains the key.
#     3. Put the key in USAJOBS_API_KEY below, and the same email address
#        in USAJOBS_EMAIL.
#     4. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     5. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines jobs --check --source usajobs
#        It must show a few USAJOBS postings. If it shows an error, copy the
#        key and the email address again.
#   More: docs/jobs.md#usajobs

# USAJOBS_API_KEY
#   The key for the USAJOBS job board (jobs with the United States federal
#   government).
#   Required: no. Default: empty (GU Headlines skips the USAJOBS board).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 and 2 above.
#   Secret: yes.
USAJOBS_API_KEY=

# USAJOBS_EMAIL
#   The email address that USAJOBS sent the key to. USAJOBS asks for this
#   address in each request. GU Headlines sends it only to USAJOBS. It does
#   not show it on the site.
#   Required: only together with USAJOBS_API_KEY. Default: empty.
#   Example: you@example.com
#   Secret: keep it private.
USAJOBS_EMAIL=

# --- Google Calendar key (the Legislature's calendar on the Events page) ---
#   The Guam Legislature keeps its calendar (sessions, public hearings,
#   committee meetings) in Google Calendar. GU Headlines reads it through
#   Google's Calendar API, which needs a key. The key is free: reading a
#   public calendar a few times a day stays far inside Google's free daily
#   limit, and Google does not ask for a credit card for this.
#   You need a Google account (a Gmail address works).
#   How to get the key:
#     1. Open https://console.cloud.google.com/ and sign in with your Google
#        account. If Google asks you to accept its terms, accept them.
#     2. Make a project (a folder for your settings at Google): click the
#        project list at the top of the page (it may say "Select a project"),
#        click "New project", type a name such as GU Headlines, and click
#        "Create". Wait a few seconds, then make sure the new project is
#        selected in the project list.
#     3. Turn on the Calendar API for that project: open
#        https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/calendar-json.googleapis.com
#        and click "Enable".
#     4. Make the key: open
#        https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#        click "Create credentials", then "API key". Google shows the new
#        key: a long line of letters and numbers that starts with "AIza".
#        Copy it.
#     5. Limit the key, so it is useless for anything else if someone sees
#        it: on the same page, click the key's name (or "Edit API key").
#        Under "API restrictions", choose "Restrict key", tick "Google
#        Calendar API" in the list, and click "Save".
#     6. Put the key in GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY below.
#     7. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     8. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines events --check --source legislature
#        It must show a few Legislature events. If it says "API key not
#        valid", copy the key again. If it says the API "has not been used in
#        project ... or it is disabled", do step 3 again and wait 5 minutes.
#   More: docs/events.md#google-calendar-key

# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY
#   The key for Google's Calendar API. GU Headlines uses it only to read
#   public calendars listed in config/events.yaml (google_calendar). It sends
#   the key only to Google, and never shows it on the site or in the logs.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (GU Headlines skips the Legislature's
#   calendar, but still lists the Legislature's session agendas).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 to 5 above.
#   Looks like: a line of 39 letters, numbers, dashes and underscores that
#   starts with AIza.
#   Secret: yes.
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Being a good visitor to the news sites
# More: docs/settings.md#being-a-good-visitor
# =============================================================================

# PER_HOST_DELAY
#   The number of seconds to wait between two requests to the same site. A
#   larger number is gentler to the site. To slow down only one site, set
#   request_delay on that site in config/sources.yaml.
#   Required: no. Default: 1.5. A decimal number is OK.
PER_HOST_DELAY=1.5

# SCRAPER_WORKERS
#   How many sites the worker checks at the same time. The pause in
#   PER_HOST_DELAY still applies to each site.
#   Required: no. Default: 4. Minimum: 1.
SCRAPER_WORKERS=4

# REQUEST_TIMEOUT
#   The number of seconds to wait for a site to answer. After that time, the
#   worker stops that request and tries again at the next check.
#   Required: no. Default: 25.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT=25

# RESPECT_ROBOTS_TXT
#   true: obey the robots.txt file of each site. In that file, a site tells
#   which of its pages robots (programs like the worker) can read.
#   Required: no. Default: true. Keep it true.
#   To make an exception for one site, set ignore_robots: true on that site
#   in config/sources.yaml (see docs/news-sources.md#robotstxt).
RESPECT_ROBOTS_TXT=true

# SCRAPER_USER_AGENT
#   The name that the worker gives to the news sites and the job boards in
#   each request (the "User-Agent"). The owners of those sites see it in
#   their logs. Visitors to your site do not see it.
#   Required: no. Default: Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0)
#   You can add a way to reach you, so that a site owner can ask you a
#   question before they block you. The address of your site is sufficient.
#   Example: Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0; +https://news.example.com)
#   To use it, remove the "# " at the start of the line below, and put your
#   own text after the "=". USAJOBS gets a different name, which GU
#   Headlines makes from its settings above.
# SCRAPER_USER_AGENT=Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; GUHeadlinesBot/1.0; +https://news.example.com)

# =============================================================================
# Photos
# More: docs/settings.md#photos
# =============================================================================

# STORE_IMAGES
#   true: keep a copy of the main photo of each story on this computer, made
#   smaller and saved as WebP (a compact picture format). Old stories then
#   keep their photos after the news site deletes them.
#   false: load each photo from the news site each time a person views a
#   page.
#   Required: no. Default: true.
#   Docker keeps the copies in its "media" volume (a storage area that stays
#   when the containers restart). Each photo is saved twice: a copy for
#   phones (about 30 to 60 KB) and a small copy, 360 pixels wide, for
#   computers and tablets, where the photo is drawn smaller (about 10 to
#   20 KB). Each browser loads only the one it needs, so pages load faster.
STORE_IMAGES=true

# IMAGE_MAX_WIDTH
#   The width, in pixels, of the larger saved photo copies (the ones for
#   phones). 720 looks sharp on the site, also on phones. A larger number
#   makes larger files. A change applies only to photos that the worker
#   saves after the change. The small copies are always 360 pixels wide.
#   Required: no. Default: 720.
IMAGE_MAX_WIDTH=720

# IMAGE_STORAGE_GB
#   The most disk space the saved photos may use, in GB (gigabytes; 1 GB is
#   1,000 MB). The photos grow by roughly 2 to 4 GB a year. When they use
#   more than this, the worker deletes the photos of the oldest stories
#   until they use 90% of it, so your server's disk never fills up with
#   photos. Those old stories then show the photo from the news site
#   itself, for as long as the news site still has it. New stories always
#   get their photos saved.
#   0: no limit. Photos are kept forever (watch your disk space, see
#   docs/maintenance.md#disk-space).
#   Required: no. Default: 5. Decimals are fine, for example 2.5.
#   How to pick a number: look at how big your server's disk is (your
#   server provider's control panel shows it, or run "df -h /" on the
#   server and read the "Size" column of the first line). Leave at least
#   half of the disk for everything else. On a 20 GB disk, 5 is fine; on a
#   60 GB disk, you could use 20.
#   How to see how much space the photos use now:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines photos
#   It shows the space used and the limit, and does the tidy-up at once
#   (the worker also does it by itself after each news check).
IMAGE_STORAGE_GB=5

# =============================================================================
# Alerts in Discord
# More: docs/maintenance.md#alerts-in-discord
# =============================================================================
# GU Headlines can post a message in a Discord channel when something needs
# your attention. Discord is a free chat app for phones and computers, and
# the Discord phone app shows each message as a notification. Alerts come:
#   - when a news site, job board or event calendar has not worked for a
#     day, and when it works again (the worker posts these),
#   - when a news site has gone quiet: it still answers, but has had no new
#     story for much longer than usual. Its feed or pages may have changed,
#     so the stories are no longer found. One more alert comes when it has
#     new stories again. (The worker posts these. There is nothing to set:
#     see docs/maintenance.md#source-health)
#   - when a new story mentions one of the words in ALERT_KEYWORDS (below),
#   - when the site stops answering, when the news has not been checked for
#     3 hours, when a nightly backup fails or none has been made for more
#     than a day, and after each automatic update (scripts/auto-update.sh
#     posts these; see docs/maintenance.md#automatic-updates),
#   - when a backup is downloaded from the stats page (the website posts
#     these; see "Backups" below),
#   - when the server runs low on disk space or memory, when an https
#     certificate is about to end, and when your mail server's address is on
#     a spam block list (see "Server checks" below).
# Reports come too: a money summary every morning, and a report of the week
# every Monday (see "Reports" below).
# Without DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL, no alerts are posted. Everything else works.
# While the visitor stats are on (STATS_PASSWORD), the stats page also shows
# how each source is doing, under "Source health" at the bottom.

# --- Discord webhook link ---
#   A webhook is a secret link that lets a program post messages in one
#   Discord channel. It can only post. It can't read your messages.
#   How to get the link (easiest on a computer, in the Discord app or at
#   https://discord.com/app in a web browser):
#     1. Sign in to Discord. If you have no account, make a free one at
#        https://discord.com/register
#     2. Make your own Discord server (a private group, not a computer):
#        in the column on the left, press the "+" button (Add a Server),
#        then "Create My Own", then "For me and my friends". Give it a
#        name, for example GU Headlines alerts, and press "Create". It gets
#        a channel named "general". You can use that channel.
#     3. Point at the channel name and press the gear button next to it
#        (Edit Channel). Open "Integrations", then "Webhooks", then press
#        "New Webhook".
#     4. Press the new webhook to open it. Change its name to GU Headlines
#        (the name shown on each alert). Press "Copy Webhook URL". Press
#        "Save Changes" if Discord shows that button.
#     5. Paste the link in DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL below. It looks like
#        https://discord.com/api/webhooks/1234567890123456789/AbCd...
#     6. Apply it:
#          docker compose up -d
#     7. Check it:
#          docker compose exec worker guheadlines alert-test
#        A test message shows in the channel within a few seconds. If you
#        see an error instead, copy the link again (step 4).
#     8. Get the alerts on your phone: install the Discord app on your
#        phone and sign in. Press and hold your server's icon, open
#        "Notifications" and choose "All Messages". Discord sends phone
#        notifications only while you are not using Discord on a computer.
#   More: docs/maintenance.md#alerts-in-discord

# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL
#   The webhook link of the Discord channel that gets the alerts.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no alerts).
#   Where to get it: steps 1 to 5 above.
#   Secret: yes. A person who has the link can post messages in your
#   channel. If other persons learn it, delete the webhook (step 3), make a
#   new one, and put the new link here.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ALERT_AFTER_HOURS
#   How many hours a news site, job board or event calendar must fail
#   before the alert comes. Sites have short problems now and then (a slow
#   server, a restart), and these fix themselves. A day (24) leaves time for
#   that. A site that fails for a day probably changed, or now turns
#   automated visitors away.
#   Required: no. Default: 24. Minimum: 1.
ALERT_AFTER_HOURS=24

# ALERT_KEYWORDS
#   Words that you want to hear about. When a new story's headline or intro
#   (its first lines) mentions one of these words, a message comes in your
#   Discord channel with the headline, the news site, the word found and the
#   link to the story. Each story comes only once. When several stories
#   come in the same check, they come together in one message.
#   This needs DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL (above).
#   How to write the words:
#     - Put a comma between them. A word can also be a phrase of several
#       words, such as power outage. Then those words must be next to each
#       other, in that order.
#     - Upper and lower case don't matter. Accents don't matter either:
#       Hagatna also finds Hagåtña.
#     - Only whole words are found, also with "s" or "es" at the end:
#       shooting also finds shootings, but fire doesn't find firefighters.
#     - Put a * at the end of a word to find every word that starts with
#       it: evacuat* finds evacuate, evacuated and evacuation.
#     - No quotes are needed. Don't use a "$" sign or a "#" (see HOW TO
#       WRITE A SETTING at the top of this file).
#   Stories hidden with "muted:" in config/sources.yaml are left out, like
#   on the site. Only news from the last 2 days counts, so when you add a
#   news site, its older stories don't come.
#   After a change, apply it:
#     docker compose up -d
#   To check: "docker compose exec worker guheadlines alert-test" sends a
#   test message that names your words.
#   Required: no. Default: empty (no keyword alerts).
#   Example: typhoon, shooting, power outage, evacuat*
ALERT_KEYWORDS=

# --- Reports ---
#   The worker posts two reports in your Discord channel (they need
#   DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL above):
#   - The money summary, every morning: what people paid yesterday (a Guam
#     day), and what waits for you to approve.
#       • Paid listings (classifieds, featured jobs and events, business
#         upgrades) and booked ads (sponsor spot, newsletter sponsor line).
#       • Bid alert payments, and new bid alert subscribers.
#       • Donations ("Buy me a coffee") and any other money that came into
#         your PayPal account, and money you gave back (refunds). For these,
#         PayPal must let the site read your account's payments: see "How to
#         turn on Transaction search" below. Without it, the summary shows
#         the rest.
#       • "Waiting for you": listings to approve (/admin/listings) and ads
#         to approve (/advertise/review), with the links.
#     It comes only while PayPal is set up (PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID above). It
#     comes without a phone notification, unless something waits for you.
#   - The weekly report, every Monday: the week from Monday to Sunday, and
#     how each number compares with the week before.
#       • Page views and story clicks, the busiest day, the most clicked
#         story, and the websites readers came from (Google, Facebook...).
#         These need the visitor stats (STATS_PASSWORD above).
#       • Clicks on the sponsor spot and on booked ads, clicks on the
#         stories in the newsletter, newsletter sign-ups on the site, and new
#         bid alert subscribers.
#       • The money of the week, as in the money summary.
#     AdSense and affiliate earnings are not in the reports. You see them
#     only when you sign in to AdSense and to each partner's website.
#   To see a report now, without waiting:
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines report daily
#     docker compose exec worker guheadlines report weekly
#   These post the report in Discord. Add --show at the end to see it in
#   the terminal only.
#   After you change one of the three settings below, apply it:
#     docker compose up -d
#
#   How to turn on Transaction search (for donations and refunds):
#     1. Open https://developer.paypal.com and sign in (as in "PayPal" above).
#     2. Click "Apps & Credentials". Put the switch at the top on the same
#        side as your PAYPAL_MODE ("Live" for real payments).
#     3. Click the name of your app (the one whose Client ID is in
#        PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID).
#     4. Scroll down to "Features". Tick "Transaction search" (it can be
#        under "Other features"). Click "Save Changes".
#     5. Restart the worker, so that it asks PayPal for the new permission:
#          docker compose restart worker
#        PayPal can take a few hours to switch it on.
#   Transaction search only lets the site read your payments. It can't send
#   money.

# REPORT_TIME
#   The time of day (Guam time) when the reports are posted: the money
#   summary every day, and the weekly report on WEEKLY_REPORT_DAY. Write it
#   as hours:minutes on a 24-hour clock (8:00, 18:30), or with am or pm
#   (8:00 am, 6:30 pm).
#   Required: no. Default: 8:00.
REPORT_TIME=8:00

# DAILY_REPORT
#   true: post the money summary every morning. false: don't.
#   Required: no. Default: true.
DAILY_REPORT=true

# WEEKLY_REPORT_DAY
#   The day of the weekly report, in English: monday, tuesday... (or the
#   first three letters: mon, tue...). The report covers the 7 days before
#   that day. Write off for no weekly report.
#   Required: no. Default: monday.
WEEKLY_REPORT_DAY=monday

# --- Server checks ---
#   The server checks itself every 5 minutes and posts an alert when
#   something runs low. scripts/auto-update.sh does this (see "Automatic
#   updates" in docs/maintenance.md), so the checks run once automatic
#   updates are on. To see the readings now, run this in the GU-headlines
#   folder on the server:
#     ./scripts/server-check.sh
#   The checks:
#   - Disk space (DISK_ALERT_PERCENT below). When the disk is full, the
#     database can't save new stories, and the site can stop.
#   - Memory (MEMORY_ALERT_PERCENT below). When the memory runs out, Linux
#     stops programs, such as the database.
#   - The https certificates (CERT_ALERT_DAYS below): the site's, and the
#     mail server's when your own mail server is on. A certificate lets
#     browsers and mail apps check that they talk to your server. Caddy
#     gets a new one by itself about 30 days before the old one ends.
#   - Spam block lists, once a day, when your own mail server is on. A
#     block list names IP addresses that sent spam. Mail services check
#     these lists, and mail from a listed address often goes to spam. There
#     is nothing to set. The alert names each list and its removal page.
#   - Once a week (Sunday night, Guam time) it deletes what Docker leaves
#     behind after updates: old versions of the program and old build
#     files. Your stories, photos, mail and database are never touched.
#     To do it now: ./scripts/server-check.sh --clean
#   Each problem is posted once, and once more when it is fixed. These
#   settings are read by the script on the server, at the next check; there
#   is no need to restart anything after a change.

# DISK_ALERT_PERCENT
#   How full the disk may get, in percent, before the alert comes. 90 means:
#   an alert when 90% of the disk is used (10% is still free).
#   Required: no. Default: 90. 0 turns the disk check off.
DISK_ALERT_PERCENT=90

# MEMORY_ALERT_PERCENT
#   How much of the memory may be in use, in percent, before the alert
#   comes. It must stay that high for about 15 minutes (three checks in a
#   row), because an update uses a lot of memory for a few minutes.
#   Required: no. Default: 90. 0 turns the memory check off.
MEMORY_ALERT_PERCENT=90

# CERT_ALERT_DAYS
#   The alert comes when a certificate ends within this many days. Caddy
#   renews about 30 days before the end, so with 14, the alert means that
#   renewing has failed for about two weeks. That leaves two weeks to fix
#   it before visitors see a warning.
#   Required: no. Default: 14. 0 turns the certificate check off.
CERT_ALERT_DAYS=14

# =============================================================================
# Backups
# More: docs/maintenance.md#backups
# =============================================================================
# Every night, the "backup" part of GU Headlines (the fifth container, next
# to db, web, worker and caddy) backs up the whole site by itself:
#   - the database (every story, job, event, bid, listing, booking and
#     subscriber, and the visitor counts),
#   - the saved photos,
#   - this .env file (your settings, passwords and keys),
#   - the config folder (news sites, topics, your sponsor picture),
#   - Caddy's https certificates,
#   - with your own email server on: the mailboxes, the mail accounts and
#     the DKIM key, and the webmail's settings.
# Each backup is a folder in backups/site in the GU-headlines folder, named
# by its date and time, for example backups/site/2026-11-26-0300. A photo
# that is in the backups of several nights is stored only once, so the
# backups of 14 nights need much less space than 14 copies of the site.
# There is nothing to turn on, and no account or key is needed.
# The backups stay on the server. To keep a copy somewhere else (a good
# idea, as a broken disk takes the backups with it), download one now and
# then:
#   - On the stats page (/stats, needs STATS_PASSWORD), under "Backups":
#     press "Download". This works only over https (with SITE_DOMAIN set)
#     and with a STATS_PASSWORD of at least 12 letters and digits, because a
#     backup holds all your passwords. Each download is posted in your
#     Discord channel (with DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL set), so you notice a
#     download that wasn't you.
#   - Or on the server: ./scripts/site-backup.sh export
#     It makes a fresh backup as one file in the backups folder, and says
#     how to copy it to your computer.
# To put a backup back (on the same server, or on a new one):
#   ./scripts/site-backup.sh restore site-backup-2026-11-26-0300.tar
# Step by step: docs/maintenance.md#restoring-from-a-backup
# If a nightly backup fails (for example when the disk is nearly full), the
# stats page says so, and scripts/auto-update.sh posts an alert in Discord.
# After you change a setting below, apply it:
#   docker compose up -d

# BACKUP_TIME
#   The time of night when the backup runs, in Guam time (ChST), on a
#   24-hour clock: 03:00 is 3 in the morning. Choose a quiet hour. A backup
#   takes from a few seconds to a few minutes, and the site keeps working
#   during it.
#   Required: no. Default: 03:00
#   Example: 02:30
#   Empty: no nightly backups. (You can still back up by hand with
#   ./scripts/site-backup.sh now.) A value that is not a time like 03:00
#   counts as 03:00.
BACKUP_TIME=03:00

# BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS
#   How many days each nightly backup is kept. Older backups are deleted
#   after each new backup, but the newest one is never deleted. Each night
#   adds about the size of the database (and the mailboxes), plus the new
#   photos of that day. If the disk gets full, make this number smaller.
#   A backup is skipped (and the stats page says so) when it would leave
#   less than 1 GB free on the disk, because the database stops working
#   when the disk is full.
#   To see how much space the backups use: ./scripts/site-backup.sh list
#   Required: no. Default: 14. Minimum: 1. Maximum: 365.
BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=14

# =============================================================================
# Troubleshooting
# More: docs/settings.md#troubleshooting-output
# =============================================================================

# LOG_LEVEL
#   How much the website and the worker write to their log. To read the log
#   of the worker, run "docker compose logs worker". Use one of these values:
#     DEBUG     Each story that the worker saves or skips, and the reason.
#               Use it to find a problem with one site. It writes a lot.
#     INFO      The normal amount.
#     WARNING   Only problems.
#     ERROR     Only errors.
#   Required: no. Default: INFO.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# =============================================================================
# Running without Docker (advanced)
# More: docs/how-it-works.md#running-without-docker
# =============================================================================
# Without Docker, GU Headlines does not read this file. Set the same names as
# environment variables in your shell or in your service file instead. The
# names below are only for that case. Do not put them in .env when you use
# Docker, because they stop the containers from finding the database and
# the photos.
#   DATABASE_URL    The full address of the database, for example
#                   postgresql://guheadlines:the-password@localhost:5432/guheadlines
#                   With it, GU Headlines does not use POSTGRES_DB,
#                   POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
#   POSTGRES_HOST   The computer that runs the database. Default: localhost.
#   POSTGRES_PORT   The port of the database. Default: 5432.
#   CONFIG_DIR      The folder with sources.yaml, categories.yaml and
#                   jobs.yaml. Default: the config folder of the project.
#   MEDIA_DIR       The folder for the saved photos. Default: data/media in
#                   the project folder.
